add('N001','A. Flat cohomology and finite group schemes','The two dimensions introduced on the opening leaves',[(2,3),(3,4)],'reading question',r'“topolog. fppf”; “Regardons d’abord le”',r'fppf|fid[èe]lement|fid\. plate|cd.?p|finite group|groupe.*fini',r'''Scan 003 first defines a dimension using all \(p\)-torsion sheaves for the finite faithfully flat topology, then introduces the primed dimension using finite commutative group schemes; the exponent on the cover remains \unsure{!|'} in the frozen file. Which passage supplies the comparison through the inclusion \(G\mapsto h_G\) of representable finite group sheaves into the stated sheaf category, and through the change-of-topology morphism \((\operatorname{Spec}k)_{\mathrm{fppf}}\to(\operatorname{Spec}k)_{\mathrm{ét}}\)?''',r'''The object search in the two supplied context files yields no earlier formulation of these two dimensions; README, S19, and MPL_33_002-049 §2 supply the comparison programme, with that prior note available only as a truncated lead.''', ['MPL_33_002-049 §2'])
add('N002','A. Flat cohomology and finite group schemes','The three successive group-scheme pieces',[(3,10)],'unproved claim',r'“Tout tel groupe algébrique se … en”',r'radiciel|multiplicatif|unipotent|d[ée]viss|finite group',r'''The displayed filtration \(G'\subset G''=G^0\subset G\) is used immediately to reduce the dimension calculation to the étale, radicial unipotent, and radicial multiplicative pieces. The written reduction needs the short exact sequences of fppf sheaves attached to these inclusions, together with a construction over the actual ground field of the first subgroup \(G'\); the page supplies the filtration and the conclusion but no construction of that subgroup.''',r'''The relevant descent and SGA 3 leads are MPL_131 §2/RN001, whose excerpted prior locations are 131/002 and 131/017; these pages add this specific cohomological use of a subgroup filtration.''', ['MPL_131 §2','MPL_131 RN001'])
add('N003','A. Flat cohomology and finite group schemes','The erased quantifier in the étale dimension test',[(5,2)],'withdrawn in the same text',r'“égal à 0 ou 1”; deleted “toute”',r'Artin.Schreier|extension.*[ée]tale|cohomological dimension|dimension cohomologique',r'''The opening sentence ties the value \(0\) or \(1\) to degrees of finite étale extensions, while “toute” is visibly cancelled and “finie” inserted on this same scan. The Artin–Schreier map \(x\mapsto x^p-x\), with kernel \(\mathbf Z/p\), gives the relevant coefficient sequence [P12]; what quantifier governs the surviving phrase “extension finie étale” in the proposed test?''',r'''The supplied context contains no earlier version of this sentence; its étale-cohomology placement in README and MPL_33_002-049 §2 is restated, while the cancellation adds a local drafting question.''',['MPL_33_002-049 §2'],['P12'])
add('N004','A. Flat cohomology and finite group schemes','Top-degree divisibility in the torus test',[(5,17),(7,2)],'unproved claim',r'“nul pour i>n, et p-divisible si i=n”',r'tore|torus|divisib|multiplicatif|norm',r'''Lemma 2 uses both higher vanishing and divisibility in the last degree, through the isogeny \([p]:T\to T\) and its kernel \({}_pT\). The two displayed fragments on scan 007 identify the kernel and cokernel terms that must enter the long exact sequence; which precise degree-\(n\) cokernel is eliminated in the sufficiency argument?''',r'''MPL_60_036-070 §2 mentions averaging and a restricted rational norm only in an excerpt (prior scans 037–047 and an unprinted later locator); the current torus-isogeny calculation adds a concrete map for comparison, without an identification of that earlier norm.''',['MPL_60_036-070 §2'])
add('N005','A. Flat cohomology and finite group schemes','The prime-to-p extension reduction',[(5,23),(7,30),(9,2)],'unproved claim',r'“il suffit d’ordre premier à p”; “est surjectif”',r'premier.*p|prime.to|norm|transfert|restriction',r'''The insertion on scan 005 tests multiplicative cohomology over finite separable extensions, and scans 007–009 use the map \(\operatorname{Res}_{k'/k}G_{k'}\to G\) to reduce further. A complete account must specify this map as a norm, its kernel in the same coefficient category, and the induced cohomology map used to obtain the displayed surjection.''',r'''The search finds the norm/averaging leads MPL_60_036-070 §2 and MPL_11_043-058+56 N045 (56/020), but neither excerpt provides this restriction-of-scalars argument; the connection is the explicitly proposed norm map, with both earlier entries retaining lead status.''',['MPL_60_036-070 §2','MPL_11_043-058+56 N045'])
add('N006','A. Flat cohomology and finite group schemes','A cancelled route to surjectivity',[(7,10),(9,10)],'withdrawn in the same text',r'“reste à vérifier”; deleted “C’est un hom. de gr. p-divisibles”',r'p.divisib|surject|suffisance|torsion',r'''The unfinished equality for \(H^i(G)\) and the cancelled paragraph on \(p\)-divisible groups preserve an abandoned route to \(H^n(T)\to H^n(T')\) being surjective. Scan 009 instead proceeds through a prime-to-\(p\) extension and a filtration with quotients \(\mu_p\), so the deleted argument should be studied together with that replacement and with its still abbreviated dévissage.''',r'''No context excerpt supplies either version of this proof; the package adds the local sequence of attempted and replacement steps to the S19 comparison requested in README.''',[])
add('N007','A. Flat cohomology and finite group schemes','The right-hand side of the restriction-of-scalars formula',[(7,22)],'reading question',r'“H^i(k,\prod_{k′/k}G′)\simeq H^i(k,G)”',r'Weil|restriction of scalars|restriction.*scalaire|Shapiro|produit',r'''The frozen display has \(H^i(k,G)\) on the right, although its preceding sentence introduces \(G'\) over \(k'\). Does the intended comparison use the adjunction counit \(\operatorname{Res}_{k'/k}G'\times_k k'\to G'\) and the target \(H^i(k',G')\), and where is that comparison proved for the topology used here?''',r'''The supplied context has no same-formula hit or alternative reading; the proposed target is a research reading question prompted by the displayed domains, and leaves the frozen text unchanged.''',[])
add('N008','A. Flat cohomology and finite group schemes','The filtration by mu-p',[(9,10)],'unproved claim',r'“ce qui implique que G se dévisse … à \mu_p”',r'mu.?p|μ|multiplicatif|p.extension|class.two|residue',r'''The underlined assertion passes from splitting by a \(p\)-extension to a filtration whose group-scheme quotients are \(\mu_p\). Which filtration of the Cartier-dual finite Galois module, and which dual short exact sequences, realize each step over \(k'\)?''',r'''LM7_PUSH C.6 is marked Proved by its return for a class-two residue problem, but its excerpt gives neither these group schemes nor a Cartier-dual filtration; this item therefore records a proof-detail request rather than re-deriving or extending that claimed classification.''',['LM7_PUSH C.6'])
add('N009','A. Flat cohomology and finite group schemes','The unfinished additive radicial calculation',[(9,22)],'unproved claim',r'“un groupe radiciel additif se dévisse en groupes \alpha_p”',r'alpha|α|radiciel|Frobenius|parfait|imparfait',r'''Lemma 3 gives values \(0\) and \(1\) according to perfection, and its explanation ends immediately after the \(\alpha_p\) dévissage. The missing calculation is attached to \(F:\mathbf G_a\to\mathbf G_a,\ x\mapsto x^p\), its fppf kernel \(\alpha_p\), and the quotient \(k/k^p\); which acyclicity statement and which filtration justify carrying that calculation to every radicial additive group on the page?''',r'''The supplied context has no excerpt about \(\alpha_p\) or this perfection test, so there is no earlier result here to cite as settling the unfinished argument.''',[])

add('N010','B. Reused SGA 3 typescripts','Finite torsion as a rigidity mechanism',[(4,18),(6,2)],'unproved claim',r'“Homomorphismes centraux”; “Ceci étant vrai pour tout m”',r'central|multiplicatif|SGA 3|descent|descente',r'''The proof compares \(u,v:H\to G\) first modulo powers of the maximal ideal, then on finite torsion subgroups \({}_mH\), and finally on all of \(H\). SGA 3 IX §5.1 is the close printed comparator [P03]; the actual bridges to check are completion, schematic density of the torsion, and descent of equality along the displayed base changes.''',r'''This supplies a concrete SGA 3 proof mechanism for MPL_131 §2, a first-pass lead about Murre and auxiliary criteria, and for RN001 at 131/002:13 and 131/017:25; only those excerpted locations are available.''',['MPL_131 §2','MPL_131 RN001'],['P03'])
add('N011','B. Reused SGA 3 typescripts','Descent to a finite stage',[(6,9)],'unproved claim',r'“u_j sera central pour j grand”',r'finite.*stage|limite|descent|descente|présentation|presentation',r'''The affine proof descends \(G\), \(H=D_S(M)\), and \(u,v\) to finitely generated subalgebras \(A_j\subset A\), then asserts eventual centrality of \(u_j\). Which finite presentation of the commutator morphism \(H_j\times G_j\to G_j\), compared with the unit section, gives the stage at which centrality descends?''',r'''MPL_131 10 and RN001 concern descent and representable functors as first-pass leads; the present scan adds this explicit equality-of-morphisms test, while their full proofs are absent from the supplied context.''',['MPL_131 10','MPL_131 RN001'])
add('N012','B. Reused SGA 3 typescripts','Repeated H in the finite-presentation hypothesis',[(6,19),(8,17)],'reading question',r'“H de présentation finie”; “comme H est séparé”',r'SGA 3|homomorph|sépar|separ|finite.presentation',r'''The frozen Corollary 5.2 repeats \(H\) in the finite-presentation hypothesis, and the kernel argument on scan 008 invokes separatedness of \(H\), while its statement assumes separatedness of \(G\). The maps to inspect are \(H\xrightarrow{u}G\) and the pullback \(\ker u=H\times_{G,e}S\); SGA 3 IX §§5.2–5.3 provides a textual comparison [P03], whereas the explicit \(H/G\) editorial correction found in SGA 3 X concerns its own Theorem 5.6 [P04].''',r'''MPL_131 §2 gives a general SGA 3 comparison lead but no reading of these two lines, so neither letter in the frozen fragment is altered on that basis.''',['MPL_131 §2'],['P03','P04'])
add('N013','B. Reused SGA 3 typescripts','The struck subgroup formulation',[(6,24),(8,2)],'withdrawn in the same text',r'“graphes des homomorphismes”; deleted “Théorème 5.1. bis”',r'graphe|graph|sous.groupe|SGA 3|homomorph',r'''The subgroup theorem and two “bis” headings are cancelled on scan 008, while the preceding page proposes graphs to connect the subgroup and homomorphism statements. The exact construction is \(h\mapsto(h,u(h))\) into \(H\times_SG\); SGA 3 IX §5.1 bis retains the corresponding subgroup formulation [P03], so the cancellation records a local drafting state whose placement needs comparison with print.''',r'''The context supplies only the broad SGA 3 lead MPL_131 §2 and no earlier copy of this cancelled passage; the current scans add its graph construction and deletion state.''',['MPL_131 §2'],['P03'])
add('N014','B. Reused SGA 3 typescripts','Open and closed Isom inside Hom',[(18,10)],'unproved claim',r'“ouvert et fermé”; “Cela résulte de 5.8.”',r'Isom|Hom|représent|represent|Murre',r'''The insertion “et fermé” strengthens the stated representation of \(\underline{\operatorname{Isom}}(H,G)\hookrightarrow\underline{\operatorname{Hom}}(H,G)\), followed by the automorphism-group conclusion. SGA 3 X §§5.8–5.10 preserves this comparison [P04]; the missing local calculation on the reused leaf is the criterion on the dual finite-type character groups that selects the invertible homomorphisms.''',r'''MPL_131 10/§2/RN001 concern representability and descent, with only 131/002 and 131/017 explicitly located; the present page adds this precise representing inclusion to those leads.''',['MPL_131 10','MPL_131 §2','MPL_131 RN001'],['P04'])
add('N015','B. Reused SGA 3 typescripts','The altered isotriviality criterion',[(18,18),(16,27),(14,4)],'reading question',r'“du moins si S est quasi-compact”; “S′ … fini étale surjectif”',r'isotriv|quasi.compact|SGA 3|descent|descente',r'''The proposition changes a disjoint sum to a union of clopen finite pieces and adds restrictions on \(S\); the proof forms a finite étale cover from selected components of the Isom scheme. SGA 3 X §5.11, editorial note 43, itself retains a verification request concerning a locally noetherian case without finite generation [P04]; which exact implication in the scan survives under each written set of hypotheses?''',r'''MPL_131 §2 is the relevant SGA 3 lead, but its excerpt gives no isotriviality criterion; these pages add the construction \(P=\underline{\operatorname{Isom}}(H,D_S(M))\to S\) and the limitations marked during drafting.''',['MPL_131 §2'],['P04'])
add('N016','B. Reused SGA 3 typescripts','Quasi-compactness replacing finite type',[(16,4)],'withdrawn in the same text',r'deleted “de type fini”; inserted “quasi-compact”',r'quasi.compact|finitude|finite|constant tordu',r'''Lemma 5.12 changes the hypothesis on \(Z\hookrightarrow R\) to quasi-compactness and reduces to a closed immersion \(Z\hookrightarrow J_S\hookrightarrow I_S\) with \(J\subset I\) finite. That finite subset and the descent of the resulting finite morphism supply the proof mechanism, also located at SGA 3 X §5.12 [P04].''',r'''No same-lemma excerpt occurs in the supplied context; the result contributes a specific finiteness step to the broader representability lead MPL_131 §2.''',['MPL_131 §2'],['P04'])
add('N017','B. Reused SGA 3 typescripts','Finiteness detected on one fiber',[(14,19),(12,5)],'unproved claim',r'“de nature locale”; “quasi-isotrivialité”',r'fibre|fiber|[ée]tale|constant tordu|isotriv',r'''Lemma 5.13 proves finiteness of a clopen \(Z\subset P\) by studying the locus where its fiber is finite and passing to an étale cover on which \(P=I_S\). The proof explicitly uses preservation of local noetherianity under that cover, and the continuation on scan 012 uses connected components of the base; the fiberwise-to-global step is located in SGA 3 X §5.13 [P04].''',r'''The context’s MPL_131 §2 comparison lead gives no fiberwise version, so the added evidence is this specific local trivialization and its stated use of quasi-isotriviality.''',['MPL_131 §2'],['P04'])
add('N018','B. Reused SGA 3 typescripts','The countable clopen exhaustion',[(14,14)],'unproved claim',r'“réunion d’une suite”; “aucune restriction sur S”',r'suite|countab|isotriv|SGA 3',r'''The aside proposes a countable clopen finite exhaustion of the character object as an isotriviality criterion after the earlier restriction on \(S\) is removed. SGA 3 X §5.11 bis and editorial note 44 retain a question about the converse [P04]; which finite étale trivializing cover is constructed from the entire written sequence?''',r'''The supplied context has no earlier version of this criterion; MPL_131 §2 supplies the broad comparison destination but cannot certify this converse from its excerpt.''',['MPL_131 §2'],['P04'])
add('N019','B. Reused SGA 3 typescripts','The geometrically unibranch extension',[(12,26)],'unproved claim',r'“géométriquement unibranche au lieu de normal”',r'normal|unibranch|SGA 3|isotriv',r'''The remark explicitly uses the normalization morphism \(\widetilde S\to S\) of \(S_{\mathrm{réd}}\), described there as radicial, to extend the preceding argument. SGA 3 X editorial notes 45–46 explain the incorporation of original Remark 5.15 into the revised §§5.14 and 5.16 [P04], giving a direct printed destination for this source assertion.''',r'''MPL_131 §2 remains a general SGA 3 lead; this package adds the actual normalization map and the original placement of the extension within the cancelled typescript.''',['MPL_131 §2'],['P04'])
add('N020','B. Reused SGA 3 typescripts','P and R through the cancelled proof',[(12,10),(12,35)],'reading question',r'inserted “P”; surviving “Comme R est plat”',r'normal|Isom|notation|SGA 3|descent',r'''The proof changes several occurrences of \(R\) to \(P\), leaves later \(R_s,R_i\) in place, and ends with competing references involving Isom and the dual character object. Which map identifies the object whose connected components are being proved finite with the later \(P=\underline{\operatorname{Isom}}(H,D_S(M))\), and which of the amended lemma numbers governs that application?''',r'''No alternative reading of these letters is present in the context; MPL_131 §2 supports consulting SGA 3, while the frozen mixed lettering remains the documentary evidence.''',['MPL_131 §2'])

add('N021','C. The module construction on scans 011–017','The meaning of fA-cofini',[(11,16)],'reading question',r'“fA-cofini”; “M/fM n’est pas fA-cofini”',r'cofini|cofinite|local cohomology|cohomologie locale|module',r'''The announced module is supported on \(V(f)\), has successive \(f\)-annihilator quotients isomorphic to \(B=A/fA\), and has \(M/fM\simeq(B/gB)^{(\mathbf N)}\). Which definition of “\(fA\)-cofini” is intended here, and which exact module maps or Ext groups certify the first occurrence while testing the second?''',r'''The two supplied context files contain no definition or earlier example of this term; the neighbouring SGA 2 typescripts on scans 026–032 provide a local cohomology setting within this package, but no definition is inserted into the transcription.''',[])
add('N022','C. The module construction on scans 011–017','An explicit Ext calculation to test the terminology',[(11,22),(15,7)],'own idea',r'“extension … de A/fA par E_n”',r'Ext|cofini|cofinite|module|extension',r'''For the concrete ring \(A=\mathbf C[[x,y]]\), \(f=x\), the free resolution \(0\to A\xrightarrow{x}A\to A/(x)\to0\) gives the cochain complex \(M\xrightarrow{x}M\), with \(H^0={}_xM\) and \(H^1=M/xM\). Does comparing these two groups for the constructed \(M\) locate the historical definition of cofiniteness intended on scan 011, especially its treatment of the infinite direct sum?''',r'''No cofinite/Ext definition is supplied by the context, so this is a proposed test of the manuscript’s terminology, not a novelty claim or an asserted replacement definition.''',[],confidence='high for the displayed two-term computation; unresolved for the historical terminology',reason='The maps and both cohomology groups are explicit, while the source leaves the term undefined.')
add('N023','C. The module construction on scans 011–017','Logical sequence across the physical ordering',[(11,28),(15,2),(13,2),(17,2)],'unpursued implication',r'“Construction”; “Vérifions les propriétés”; “De plus”',r'construction|module|page|pagination|feuille',r'''The construction announced on scan 011 is defined by the pushout on scan 015, checked by the equations on scan 013, and passed to a direct limit on scan 017. This dependency order \(011\to015\to013\to017\) follows the displayed objects \(E_n,E_{n+1},Q_n,M\) and should accompany mathematical study, while the diplomatic fragments retain the physical scan order.''',r'''The page-accounting leads MPL_33_002-049 §93 and MPL_33_147-181+34_002-017 8.3 establish only an earlier reporting practice in the excerpts; this package adds its own explicit dependency chain and dual manuscript numerals.''',['MPL_33_002-049 §93','MPL_33_147-181+34_002-017 8.3'])
add('N024','C. The module construction on scans 011–017','The annihilator inference involving eta-n',[(13,19),(15,26)],'unproved claim',r'“\eta_n\ne0, donc”; “\dot\xi_n=(\dot g,0)”',r'annihil|régulier|regular|cofini|module',r'''The written implication moves from \(\eta_n\ne0\) in \(B\) to the conclusion \(h\in fA\) from \(h\eta_n=0\), while the preceding construction chooses \(\bar\xi_n=(\bar g,0)\) with \(\bar g\) regular. Which identification \({}_fE_n\simeq B\) carries \(\eta_n=f^{n-1}\xi_n\) to a regular element, and how is that identification preserved by the pushout maps?''',r'''No context excerpt settles this annihilator calculation; the current pages supply both the regularity hypothesis and the exact place where its use needs to be made explicit.''',[])
add('N025','C. The module construction on scans 011–017','A concrete presentation for the regularity step',[(13,19),(15,2)],'own idea',r'“f^{n-1}\xi_n”; “extension E_{n+1}”',r'cofini|annihil|regular|module|extension',r'''In \(A=\mathbf C[[x,y]]\), consider \(E_n=A^n/(xe_1,xe_2+ye_1,\ldots,xe_n+ye_{n-1})\) and \(\xi_n=ye_n\), so \(x^{n-1}\xi_n=(-1)^{n-1}y^ne_1\). Comparing coefficients from \(e_n\) down to \(e_2\) in a relation \(he_1\in(xe_1,xe_2+ye_1,\ldots)\) gives \(\operatorname{Ann}(e_1)=(x)\); since \(y^n\) has nonzero image in the domain \(A/(x)\), the annihilator of \(y^ne_1\) is also \((x)\). Does this presentation track the manuscript’s pushout and chosen identifications closely enough to expose the regular-element argument behind scan 013?''',r'''The context has no presentation of these modules, so the calculation is offered as a concrete comparison for this package alone.''',[],confidence='high for the concrete calculation; medium for identification with every choice in the draft',reason='The triangular relations prove the stated annihilator directly; matching all pushout identifications still needs to be written.')
add('N026','C. The module construction on scans 011–017','The direct-limit basis argument',[(17,2)],'unproved claim',r'“appliquant une base sur une base”; “M satisfait aux conditions voulues”',r'limite inductive|direct limit|base|cofini|module',r'''The last leaf claims that the transition \(Q_n\to Q_{n+1}\) kills \(gQ_n\) and induces a map taking a basis to part of a basis after reduction modulo \(g\). The required comparison is the actual induced map \(Q_n/gQ_n\to Q_{n+1}/gQ_{n+1}\), together with the compatible embeddings \(E_n\to E_{n+1}\); recording these matrices would make the countable direct-sum conclusion checkable.''',r'''No same-construction hit occurs in the supplied context; the page adds a concrete limit argument whose basis compatibility is asserted but not displayed.''',[])
